.TH CHMAP_GET_ELEM_COPY 3 "2026-09-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_get_elem_copy \- copy the value associated with a key out of a hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_get_elem_copy(chmap chm, const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                  void *target_buf, size_t target_buf_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Looks key_pair up in the map and copies min(value_size,
target_buf_size) bytes of the value into target_buf, so a buffer that is
too small gets a partial copy and not an error. When target_buf_size is
larger than the stored value, the rest of target_buf is set to zero.
cbmap_get_elem_copy(3) is stricter and needs the sizes to match
exactly.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm              Hash map to search
key_pair         Key to look up
target_buf       Buffer that receives the copy of the value
target_buf_size  Size of target_buf
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - The key was found and its value copied into
                      target_buf
ccol_key_not_found  - The key is not in the map
ccol_invalid_args   - A pointer is NULL, a size is 0, or key_pair's
                      size does not match the size of a fixed-width
                      key type (see chmap_create_full(3))
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
key_pair->ptr needs no particular alignment, because the map hashes and
compares the key bytes through memcpy, never through a pointer cast to the
key type. So it can point straight at key bytes at any offset in a larger
buffer, such as a field of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped
record, and you don't have to copy them into an aligned local variable
first
O(1) average complexity
Open addressing: O(n) worst case for linear probing
Separate chaining: O(n) worst case per bucket, where n is chain length
Copies min(actual_value_size, target_buf_size) bytes
Safe with an undersized buffer, which gets a partial copy
Bytes of target_buf beyond the copied value are zeroed
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_get_elem_copy (3)
